{"title":"2012 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 12","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2012 AMC 12A Problems\">2012 AMC 12A #9</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2012 AMC 10A Problems\">2012 AMC 10A #12</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Remark\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remark</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A year is a leap year if and only if the year number is divisible by 400 (such as 2000) or is divisible by 4 but not 100 (such as 2012).  The 200th anniversary of the birth of novelist Charles Dickens was celebrated on February 7, 2012, a Tuesday.  On what day of the week was Dickens born?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/3/ed37611cc15dc4910978f7649b0f147f0bf77367.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\text{Friday}\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\text{Saturday}\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\text{Sunday}\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\text{Monday}\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\text{Tuesday}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"587\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In this solution we refer to moving to the left as decreasing the year or date number and moving to the right as  increasing the year or date number. Every non-leap year we move to the right results in moving one day to the right because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/5/5459ff4c1c759d42d3ae03ae437da66b0998fc42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$365\\equiv 1\\pmod 7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"131\" height=\"18\" />.  Every leap year we move to the right results in moving <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> days to the right since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/3/42373d05b32e269f63f6fa9abd47c3108081d34a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$366\\equiv 2\\pmod 7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"131\" height=\"18\" />. A leap year is usually every four years, so 200 years would have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/5/cf5eca5564bf1a03666df2e7ed5a72af5d4cc740.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{200}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"29\" height=\"38\" /> = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906734e2f1010e9a171ffee8050cbc0649cea37c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$50$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> leap years, but the problem says that 1900 does not count as a leap year.\n</p><p>Therefore there would be 151 regular years and 49 leap years, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/f/5df0a401ea3197efc2220805bea1b15aed619b3d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1(151)+2(49)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"112\" height=\"18\" /> = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/7/8f70a88401805d41dd703dc15c65ff8880289e86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$249$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> days back.  Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/c/b8c220a15bcbb87e5e1910bb635b6cfb3edd5968.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$249 \\equiv 4\\ (\\text{mod}\\ 7)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"125\" height=\"18\" />, four days back from Tuesday would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/0/c00f5a7587ce2ee018577d5509f333f896ee6940.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\text{Friday}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"93\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since they say that February <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />th, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/8/f48f95ec89b6c50220ecbbbb6130586135db8ff6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2012$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> is the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/3/8f37626d49b4082c9102bf930bca51277ff2eb43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$200$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />th anniversary of Charles Dickens birthday, that means that the birth of Charles Dickens is on February <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />th, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/0/1b09c6befdbb9f056cbd25146b93547033aaaebc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1812$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" />. We then see that there is a leap year on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/9/759fdc29ba6d8259afa6b473cc8dacfa4086fbe6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1812, 1816, ...., 2008$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"155\" height=\"17\" /> but we must exclude <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/1/cb188438cd378732afbc6cc5d4845e38792bd7fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1900$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> which equates to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/c/34c8e6bf4d57cd6f2bb0d7d43e00f0f442f53b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$49$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" /> leap years. So, the amount of days we have to go back is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/0/690374600847fe6b85660fe19d879d3892edb1d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$200(365) + 49$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"111\" height=\"20\" /> days which in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/8/fd857d6a0ef1f81bc60305f76c5c39f1194b40d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{mod }7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" /> gives us 4. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> days back from Tuesday is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/0/c00f5a7587ce2ee018577d5509f333f896ee6940.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\text{Friday}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"93\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remark\">Remark</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Remark\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The reason why there are 49 leap years:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/a/55a64b33750afe51aa0bbd57df78838b96ddd094.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\quad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"2\" /> There are 200 <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/8/1c81486a525ba08192aab8546dbe9a32bdbe0c07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\div$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> 4  years which are divisible by 4. There are 2 years which are divisible by 100 within these 200 years. There is 1 year which is divisible by 400. Therefore , there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/b/e5b77ba7b778ea1c0bd05c0869a2fd6751070d46.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{200}{4} -2 + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"96\" height=\"38\" /> leap years.\n</p><p>~ JiYang\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; w